WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:08.039
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.930
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.750
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.469
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:17.080
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.130
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:20.869
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:24.880
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's the

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.970
start of another three-part mini-series,

00:00:28.259 --> 00:00:31.000
this time on the nine centuries of

00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:34.050
struggle between Ireland and England.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.729
In today's episode, we go back to the

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.140
very beginning: the moment when the

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.000
first English-backed soldiers stepped

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:46.080
ashore in Ireland — and how what began

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.340
as a temporary military favour became,

00:00:49.389 --> 00:00:51.400
over the following five centuries, a

00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:54.380
permanent and deeply painful occupation.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.940
In part two, we move to the

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.190
nineteenth century: the Act of

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.900
Union, the catastrophic Great Famine,

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.270
and how the relationship broke

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.570
down, seemingly, beyond repair.

00:01:06.789 --> 00:01:09.329
And in part three, we will follow Ireland

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.109
through rebellion to independence.

00:01:12.502 --> 00:01:14.522
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:16.882
minute and get right into it.

00:01:17.512 --> 00:01:20.172
Now, before we go anywhere,

00:01:20.502 --> 00:01:23.332
let me paint a picture of where

00:01:23.332 --> 00:01:25.682
today's episode — and this entire

00:01:25.692 --> 00:01:28.082
mini-series — will be taking place.

00:01:28.892 --> 00:01:31.472
The British Isles, as they are sometimes

00:01:31.472 --> 00:01:34.122
called, are the group of islands

00:01:34.512 --> 00:01:37.162
off the north-west coast of Europe.

00:01:37.732 --> 00:01:40.332
The largest island is Britain, which

00:01:40.332 --> 00:01:43.242
is made up of three countries: England

00:01:43.472 --> 00:01:46.752
in the south and east, Scotland in

00:01:46.752 --> 00:01:49.722
the north, and Wales to the west.

00:01:50.422 --> 00:01:53.262
Each of those countries also includes

00:01:53.272 --> 00:01:55.492
some smaller islands, and there

00:01:55.492 --> 00:01:57.282
is the added complication of some

00:01:57.282 --> 00:01:59.322
islands that are something confusing

00:01:59.512 --> 00:02:01.712
called crown dependencies, but

00:02:01.712 --> 00:02:03.492
that's too much detail for today.

00:02:04.662 --> 00:02:08.402
Just to the west of Britain, across

00:02:08.452 --> 00:02:11.202
a stretch of water called the Irish

00:02:11.262 --> 00:02:15.062
Sea, lies an island called Ireland.

00:02:16.142 --> 00:02:17.922
It's a smaller island than Britain.

00:02:18.342 --> 00:02:21.422
It's roughly the size of Portugal.

00:02:21.932 --> 00:02:24.152
It's green, rainy, and

00:02:24.162 --> 00:02:25.472
incredibly beautiful.

00:02:26.422 --> 00:02:29.092
And today, if you look at

00:02:29.122 --> 00:02:31.482
a map of Ireland, you will

00:02:31.482 --> 00:02:33.622
notice something unusual.

00:02:34.612 --> 00:02:37.802
The island is divided into two parts.

00:02:38.272 --> 00:02:40.702
The larger part — twenty-six

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:43.762
counties in the south, west, and

00:02:43.772 --> 00:02:47.599
centre — forms the Republic of Ireland.

00:02:48.079 --> 00:02:50.479
It is an independent country and a

00:02:50.479 --> 00:02:52.879
full member of the European Union.

00:02:54.109 --> 00:02:57.989
The smaller part — the six counties in the

00:02:57.999 --> 00:03:01.655
north-east — is called Northern Ireland.

00:03:01.655 --> 00:03:03.545
It is part of the United Kingdom,

00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:05.655
alongside England, Scotland, and Wales,

00:03:06.035 --> 00:03:09.595
and it's governed ultimately from London.

00:03:10.275 --> 00:03:13.885
This, confusingly, makes Northern Ireland

00:03:14.135 --> 00:03:17.265
part of the geographical area of Ireland,

00:03:17.445 --> 00:03:21.655
the island of Ireland, but separate from

00:03:21.655 --> 00:03:24.365
the country of Ireland, the Republic

00:03:24.365 --> 00:03:26.345
of Ireland, to give it its full name.

00:03:27.755 --> 00:03:30.585
This division is not some

00:03:30.925 --> 00:03:33.205
ancient accident of geography.

00:03:33.625 --> 00:03:36.405
It is the direct result of decisions

00:03:36.465 --> 00:03:38.425
made just over a hundred years ago.

00:03:39.045 --> 00:03:42.135
And those decisions were themselves

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.805
the product of a relationship between

00:03:44.915 --> 00:03:47.265
Ireland and England — and later

00:03:47.265 --> 00:03:49.705
Ireland and Britain — a relationship

00:03:49.995 --> 00:03:52.585
that goes back nine hundred years.

00:03:53.525 --> 00:03:56.315
This is what this mini-series is about:

00:03:56.715 --> 00:03:59.605
not the complete history of Ireland,

00:03:59.795 --> 00:04:03.235
but a journey through the key events

00:04:03.425 --> 00:04:05.715
that shaped the Ireland we know today.

00:04:06.875 --> 00:04:09.125
And one more thing before we begin,

00:04:09.575 --> 00:04:12.405
let me quickly introduce the main

00:04:12.455 --> 00:04:14.975
characters — or rather, the main groups

00:04:15.265 --> 00:04:17.685
— because it can get confusing quickly.

00:04:19.005 --> 00:04:21.665
In the twelfth century, when our

00:04:21.675 --> 00:04:25.405
story begins, Ireland was home to the

00:04:25.455 --> 00:04:28.925
Gaelic Irish — a Celtic people with

00:04:28.925 --> 00:04:31.305
their own language, their own laws,

00:04:31.705 --> 00:04:34.585
and their own system of kingship.

00:04:35.915 --> 00:04:38.125
At this point, Ireland

00:04:38.375 --> 00:04:40.365
was not a unified country.

00:04:40.765 --> 00:04:43.445
It was a patchwork of kingdoms,

00:04:43.755 --> 00:04:45.315
clans, and territories.

00:04:46.205 --> 00:04:49.775
There was the High King sitting loosely

00:04:49.965 --> 00:04:53.965
at the top, but his authority was limited

00:04:54.485 --> 00:04:57.655
and often more symbolic than absolute.

00:04:58.092 --> 00:05:00.455
Irish politics was fluid,

00:05:01.015 --> 00:05:03.755
competitive, and often violent.

00:05:04.145 --> 00:05:06.215
Kings rose and fell.

00:05:06.675 --> 00:05:08.185
Alliances shifted.

00:05:09.535 --> 00:05:11.935
To the east, by the twelfth

00:05:11.935 --> 00:05:14.855
century, England had already gone

00:05:14.875 --> 00:05:17.225
through one major change of power.

00:05:18.045 --> 00:05:23.365
In 1066, a group from Normandy in northern

00:05:23.365 --> 00:05:26.625
France had invaded England under William

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:29.025
the Conqueror and taken over the country.

00:05:29.565 --> 00:05:31.659
These were the Normans.

00:05:32.649 --> 00:05:35.855
These Normans were exceptional soldiers.

00:05:36.055 --> 00:05:38.185
They spoke Norman-French.

00:05:38.265 --> 00:05:39.305
They were Catholic.

00:05:39.685 --> 00:05:43.105
And they brought with them a feudal

00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:46.065
system of land ownership quite different

00:05:46.315 --> 00:05:49.035
from anything in the Gaelic world, a

00:05:49.035 --> 00:05:51.765
system in which land was controlled

00:05:51.785 --> 00:05:55.505
through a hierarchy of lords and tenants.

00:05:56.105 --> 00:05:59.665
So when people talk about "the English"

00:05:59.935 --> 00:06:02.875
arriving in Ireland, what they really mean

00:06:03.345 --> 00:06:06.695
— at first, at least — is Anglo-Normans.

00:06:07.065 --> 00:06:09.995
The ruling class of England was Norman.

00:06:10.125 --> 00:06:12.185
The soldiers who would cross

00:06:12.185 --> 00:06:14.605
the Irish Sea were Norman.

00:06:14.915 --> 00:06:16.155
The language they brought

00:06:16.425 --> 00:06:17.765
was Norman-French.

00:06:18.015 --> 00:06:20.395
The name "English" came later.

00:06:21.445 --> 00:06:21.845
Right.

00:06:22.355 --> 00:06:24.145
Now let us begin.

00:06:25.485 --> 00:06:29.095
In 1166, a king was on the run.

00:06:30.005 --> 00:06:32.182
His name was Diarmait Mac Murchada

00:06:32.435 --> 00:06:34.875
and he was the King of Leinster.

00:06:35.615 --> 00:06:37.945
Leinster is the eastern province

00:06:37.945 --> 00:06:39.875
of Ireland, the area that

00:06:39.875 --> 00:06:41.475
includes what is now Dublin.

00:06:42.609 --> 00:06:45.165
He had been expelled by the High

00:06:45.205 --> 00:06:47.625
King of Ireland, with the backing of

00:06:47.625 --> 00:06:50.115
a rival chieftain named Tigernán Ua

00:06:50.115 --> 00:06:52.885
Ruairc — a man with a very personal

00:06:52.895 --> 00:06:54.925
reason to want Diarmait gone.

00:06:56.195 --> 00:06:58.515
Years earlier, Diarmait had

00:06:58.575 --> 00:07:00.535
abducted Tigernán's wife.

00:07:01.015 --> 00:07:04.295
And back then, like today, this wasn't

00:07:04.325 --> 00:07:07.105
something that most people took kindly

00:07:07.105 --> 00:07:10.315
to, and the grudge had never faded.

00:07:11.475 --> 00:07:14.035
When he was expelled, Diarmait

00:07:14.345 --> 00:07:16.355
did what many rulers in trouble

00:07:16.355 --> 00:07:19.305
do: he went looking for allies.

00:07:20.145 --> 00:07:22.335
He crossed to Britain, then

00:07:22.395 --> 00:07:24.015
to northern France, where he

00:07:24.015 --> 00:07:27.255
found King Henry II of England.

00:07:28.272 --> 00:07:30.845
He asked Henry for permission to

00:07:30.845 --> 00:07:33.755
recruit soldiers from among his lords.

00:07:34.505 --> 00:07:37.575
Now, Henry had no interest

00:07:37.595 --> 00:07:39.445
in invading Ireland himself.

00:07:39.915 --> 00:07:41.575
He had territories stretching

00:07:41.575 --> 00:07:43.845
from Scotland to southern France,

00:07:44.055 --> 00:07:46.105
and keeping them together was

00:07:46.135 --> 00:07:48.515
already a full-time occupation.

00:07:49.205 --> 00:07:51.745
But he gave Diarmait a letter,

00:07:52.085 --> 00:07:54.585
essentially a royal permission slip,

00:07:54.905 --> 00:07:57.485
saying that if any of his English

00:07:57.485 --> 00:08:00.585
lords wanted to help this exiled

00:08:00.675 --> 00:08:03.075
Irish king, they were free to do so.

00:08:04.159 --> 00:08:06.329
The lord who took up this offer

00:08:06.649 --> 00:08:09.109
was a man named Richard de

00:08:09.109 --> 00:08:11.139
Clare, the Earl of Pembroke.

00:08:11.859 --> 00:08:13.709
History remembers him by

00:08:13.709 --> 00:08:15.719
his nickname: Strongbow.

00:08:16.489 --> 00:08:18.849
And if you have spent much time in British

00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:21.589
pubs, you might remember this name as

00:08:21.589 --> 00:08:24.449
a type of not-exactly-delicious cider.

00:08:25.279 --> 00:08:28.389
Now, Strongbow was not doing this

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:30.369
out of the kindness of his heart.

00:08:30.739 --> 00:08:32.409
It was a business arrangement.

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:34.909
Strongbow would provide

00:08:34.959 --> 00:08:37.109
soldiers and military skill.

00:08:37.769 --> 00:08:39.919
In return, he would get Diarmait's

00:08:39.979 --> 00:08:42.899
daughter Aoife in marriage, and

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:45.319
the right to inherit the Kingdom

00:08:45.319 --> 00:08:47.439
of Leinster when Diarmait died.

00:08:48.442 --> 00:08:51.382
Strongbow landed in the south of Ireland

00:08:51.592 --> 00:08:55.312
in 1170 with around a thousand men.

00:08:56.282 --> 00:08:59.682
Within months, his men had helped capture

00:09:00.002 --> 00:09:02.332
the cities of Waterford and Dublin.

00:09:03.102 --> 00:09:05.212
A year later, Diarmait was dead,

00:09:05.502 --> 00:09:08.132
and Strongbow was King of Leinster.

00:09:09.232 --> 00:09:11.532
Shortly afterwards, Henry II

00:09:11.562 --> 00:09:14.562
arrived, and not to congratulate him.

00:09:15.592 --> 00:09:17.502
Henry was alarmed.

00:09:17.982 --> 00:09:20.832
A powerful Norman lord establishing

00:09:20.832 --> 00:09:22.832
a semi-independent kingdom in

00:09:22.832 --> 00:09:25.892
Ireland, outside his control, was

00:09:25.892 --> 00:09:27.242
the last thing he had intended.

00:09:28.272 --> 00:09:30.412
He arrived with a large fleet

00:09:30.752 --> 00:09:32.892
— reportedly around four hundred

00:09:32.892 --> 00:09:35.922
ships — and marched into Waterford.

00:09:36.905 --> 00:09:40.032
Strongbow submitted to him, and so

00:09:40.032 --> 00:09:42.472
too did a number of Irish kings.

00:09:43.302 --> 00:09:45.032
Now, why did the Irish

00:09:45.032 --> 00:09:47.092
kings submit so readily?

00:09:47.482 --> 00:09:49.242
Well, a few reasons.

00:09:50.042 --> 00:09:52.472
Firstly, Henry was the most

00:09:52.552 --> 00:09:54.682
powerful king in Western Europe.

00:09:55.022 --> 00:09:57.242
His army was far larger than

00:09:57.262 --> 00:09:58.892
anything Ireland could field.

00:09:59.612 --> 00:10:01.922
But there was something else at work too.

00:10:03.012 --> 00:10:05.772
Many Irish kings were more worried

00:10:05.902 --> 00:10:08.772
about Strongbow than about Henry.

00:10:09.972 --> 00:10:12.932
A Norman lord controlling Leinster

00:10:12.932 --> 00:10:15.932
and Dublin was a direct, immediate

00:10:15.932 --> 00:10:17.912
threat to their own territories.

00:10:18.752 --> 00:10:21.222
Henry, by contrast, was far

00:10:21.222 --> 00:10:24.042
away and apparently had no

00:10:24.052 --> 00:10:26.172
wish to rule Ireland directly.

00:10:27.072 --> 00:10:30.242
It seemed like a smarter plan to give

00:10:30.242 --> 00:10:33.972
a distant king a title on paper than to

00:10:33.972 --> 00:10:36.192
fight the Normans on their own doorstep.

00:10:37.032 --> 00:10:38.532
So they submitted.

00:10:39.642 --> 00:10:41.682
In practice, this looked like it

00:10:41.682 --> 00:10:44.412
meant very little, at first at least.

00:10:45.072 --> 00:10:48.282
Henry was not dismantling Irish kingdoms.

00:10:48.502 --> 00:10:50.032
He was not replacing Irish

00:10:50.032 --> 00:10:51.972
kings with English governors.

00:10:52.632 --> 00:10:55.532
He was simply asking them to acknowledge

00:10:55.532 --> 00:10:58.402
him as their overlord — to place him

00:10:58.592 --> 00:11:00.932
at the top of the chain of loyalty,

00:11:01.222 --> 00:11:03.502
while leaving Irish kings free to

00:11:03.502 --> 00:11:05.892
rule their own territories as before.

00:11:06.902 --> 00:11:08.632
It was, in other words,

00:11:08.922 --> 00:11:10.882
a relatively light touch.

00:11:11.922 --> 00:11:15.102
Henry took pledges of loyalty, brought

00:11:15.102 --> 00:11:17.322
Strongbow firmly under his authority,

00:11:17.622 --> 00:11:19.742
and sailed home the following year.

00:11:20.782 --> 00:11:22.712
For most of Ireland, life

00:11:22.982 --> 00:11:24.952
went on exactly as before.

00:11:25.869 --> 00:11:28.552
But something important had happened.

00:11:29.482 --> 00:11:33.502
By 1171, the English — or more

00:11:33.502 --> 00:11:36.272
precisely, the Anglo-Normans — they

00:11:36.272 --> 00:11:39.342
had established a foothold in Ireland,

00:11:39.802 --> 00:11:42.802
and they would never entirely let go.

00:11:43.962 --> 00:11:46.352
Now, you will forgive me if I skip

00:11:46.352 --> 00:11:48.622
forward by a couple of centuries here.

00:11:49.152 --> 00:11:52.112
What happened between 1170 and the late

00:11:52.112 --> 00:11:56.392
1400s is a long story — and for our

00:11:56.392 --> 00:11:59.002
purposes, the key thing to understand

00:11:59.042 --> 00:12:01.962
is this: the English presence in

00:12:02.012 --> 00:12:05.582
Ireland was real, it existed, but

00:12:05.872 --> 00:12:07.942
it was pretty limited in practice.

00:12:08.712 --> 00:12:11.662
The Normans introduced English law and

00:12:11.662 --> 00:12:14.612
a feudal system of land ownership quite

00:12:14.622 --> 00:12:17.332
different from the previous Gaelic model.

00:12:17.552 --> 00:12:19.842
They built castles, established

00:12:19.902 --> 00:12:21.732
new centres of power, and

00:12:21.762 --> 00:12:24.242
reshaped many Irish towns.

00:12:24.662 --> 00:12:26.232
But they never controlled

00:12:26.332 --> 00:12:27.292
the whole island.

00:12:27.602 --> 00:12:31.132
The area of reliable English rule, which

00:12:31.302 --> 00:12:34.192
centred on Dublin, was called the Pale.

00:12:34.952 --> 00:12:39.322
A pale, P-A-L-E, as a noun, is literally

00:12:39.322 --> 00:12:41.932
a fenced boundary, an enclosure.

00:12:42.882 --> 00:12:44.372
You might know the expression

00:12:44.682 --> 00:12:47.252
to be "beyond the pale", and

00:12:47.262 --> 00:12:48.502
this is where it comes from.

00:12:49.562 --> 00:12:52.312
Originally, to be beyond the Pale

00:12:52.542 --> 00:12:55.012
was to be outside the ordered

00:12:55.082 --> 00:12:57.832
English world — but the phrase passed

00:12:57.852 --> 00:12:59.802
into the English language to mean

00:12:59.802 --> 00:13:02.692
something unacceptable, outside the

00:13:02.762 --> 00:13:04.932
boundaries of civilised behaviour.

00:13:06.232 --> 00:13:08.812
And beyond the Pale, Gaelic Ireland

00:13:09.022 --> 00:13:10.992
continued much as before, with

00:13:10.992 --> 00:13:13.722
its own chiefs, its own laws, its

00:13:13.722 --> 00:13:15.562
own language and its own culture.

00:13:16.592 --> 00:13:18.612
And then something happened that you

00:13:18.612 --> 00:13:22.062
might think was perfectly predictable, but

00:13:22.342 --> 00:13:25.212
that took the English Crown by surprise.

00:13:26.462 --> 00:13:29.842
Many Norman families, who were living

00:13:29.862 --> 00:13:32.162
side by side with the Gaelic Irish,

00:13:32.692 --> 00:13:35.702
they gradually absorbed Irish ways.

00:13:36.042 --> 00:13:38.202
They learned the Irish language.

00:13:38.655 --> 00:13:40.805
They adopted Irish customs.

00:13:41.065 --> 00:13:43.845
They intermarried with local families.

00:13:44.225 --> 00:13:47.035
They became known as the Old English.

00:13:47.035 --> 00:13:50.335
The settlers were becoming, it was said,

00:13:50.845 --> 00:13:53.515
"more Irish than the Irish themselves."

00:13:54.635 --> 00:13:56.345
The English Crown spent

00:13:56.425 --> 00:13:58.505
decades trying to stop this.

00:13:58.905 --> 00:14:00.915
Laws were passed in the 14th century

00:14:01.195 --> 00:14:03.985
trying to ban English settlers from

00:14:03.985 --> 00:14:06.532
speaking Irish, wearing Irish clothes,

00:14:06.762 --> 00:14:08.952
or from marrying Irish people.

00:14:09.802 --> 00:14:11.782
They were largely ignored.

00:14:12.852 --> 00:14:15.472
English law in Ireland was, in other

00:14:15.472 --> 00:14:18.382
words, a paper tiger — a rule that

00:14:18.382 --> 00:14:21.902
looked powerful on paper but had no real

00:14:21.902 --> 00:14:24.082
substance when it came to enforcement.

00:14:24.892 --> 00:14:27.572
So by the early sixteenth century,

00:14:28.072 --> 00:14:30.932
meaningful English control had shrunk

00:14:30.942 --> 00:14:33.982
back to a narrow strip around Dublin.

00:14:34.712 --> 00:14:37.122
The rest of the island was governed by

00:14:37.302 --> 00:14:40.462
Gaelic and Anglo-Irish lords who paid

00:14:40.482 --> 00:14:42.902
little attention to the Crown in London.

00:14:43.772 --> 00:14:45.722
All was well and good, but

00:14:45.742 --> 00:14:48.032
then Anne Boleyn arrived.

00:14:48.442 --> 00:14:51.602
Or rather, Henry VIII decided he wanted

00:14:51.602 --> 00:14:54.112
to marry Anne Boleyn, this led him to

00:14:54.112 --> 00:14:57.202
break with Rome, and this had knock-on

00:14:57.472 --> 00:14:59.992
lasting consequences for Ireland.

00:15:01.062 --> 00:15:05.582
As a quick reminder, in 1534 Henry VIII

00:15:05.892 --> 00:15:08.222
officially broke with the Catholic Church

00:15:08.242 --> 00:15:10.632
in Rome and established the Church of

00:15:10.632 --> 00:15:13.622
England — a new, Protestant church under

00:15:13.632 --> 00:15:16.832
his own authority rather than the Pope's.

00:15:17.782 --> 00:15:20.752
He wanted Ireland to follow, but

00:15:21.012 --> 00:15:23.212
it remained stubbornly Catholic.

00:15:23.722 --> 00:15:26.322
The Irish kept their priests, their

00:15:26.332 --> 00:15:28.862
Mass, and their loyalty to Rome.

00:15:30.072 --> 00:15:32.472
For the English Crown, this

00:15:32.472 --> 00:15:34.622
wasn't just a religious insult;

00:15:34.822 --> 00:15:36.952
it was a massive security risk.

00:15:37.642 --> 00:15:39.702
In an age of Catholic superpowers

00:15:39.802 --> 00:15:42.522
like Spain and France, a Catholic

00:15:42.632 --> 00:15:45.152
Ireland was seen as a potential

00:15:45.182 --> 00:15:47.872
"backdoor," a place where England’s

00:15:47.922 --> 00:15:50.822
enemies could land an army and launch

00:15:50.972 --> 00:15:53.432
an invasion on London’s doorstep.

00:15:54.092 --> 00:15:59.012
So, in 1541, Henry VIII declared

00:15:59.012 --> 00:16:02.572
himself King of Ireland, an upgrade

00:16:02.802 --> 00:16:04.802
from the previous title of Lord.

00:16:05.552 --> 00:16:07.982
This was a clear signal that he

00:16:07.982 --> 00:16:10.502
intended to rule the entire island,

00:16:10.662 --> 00:16:12.762
not just manage it from afar.

00:16:14.192 --> 00:16:17.632
To secure this "backdoor," he launched

00:16:17.632 --> 00:16:21.098
a policy called "surrender and regrant".

00:16:22.152 --> 00:16:25.092
The idea was to bring Irish lords

00:16:25.152 --> 00:16:28.082
formally into the English legal system

00:16:28.242 --> 00:16:31.092
by making them English subjects on paper.

00:16:32.302 --> 00:16:34.492
Under this policy, they would give

00:16:34.492 --> 00:16:37.072
up their traditional Gaelic titles,

00:16:37.402 --> 00:16:40.392
acknowledge Henry as king, and receive

00:16:40.402 --> 00:16:43.272
their lands back as English-style

00:16:43.282 --> 00:16:46.222
grants with titles like Earl or Baron.

00:16:47.242 --> 00:16:49.332
Some agreed, because it seemed

00:16:49.332 --> 00:16:51.332
like a practical compromise:

00:16:51.582 --> 00:16:53.712
recognition from the Crown, while

00:16:53.712 --> 00:16:56.012
keeping their lands and local power.

00:16:56.902 --> 00:16:58.352
Many did not.

00:16:58.762 --> 00:17:01.322
And over time, those who refused

00:17:01.562 --> 00:17:03.142
would increasingly be treated

00:17:03.332 --> 00:17:05.732
not as semi-independent rulers,

00:17:05.912 --> 00:17:08.352
but as obstacles to be subdued.

00:17:09.252 --> 00:17:12.772
And those who did agree often found,

00:17:12.992 --> 00:17:14.822
in the following generations, that

00:17:14.822 --> 00:17:17.302
they had stumbled into a trap.

00:17:18.302 --> 00:17:22.002
Under Gaelic tradition, land belonged

00:17:22.072 --> 00:17:25.132
to the clan — it was managed by the lord

00:17:25.472 --> 00:17:28.392
on behalf of his people, and leadership

00:17:28.662 --> 00:17:31.312
passed not necessarily to the lord's

00:17:31.382 --> 00:17:35.112
eldest son, but to whoever the clan chose.

00:17:35.872 --> 00:17:38.862
Under English law, land was

00:17:38.862 --> 00:17:40.922
the lord's personal property,

00:17:41.262 --> 00:17:43.652
and passed to his eldest son.

00:17:44.832 --> 00:17:48.552
So when an Irish lord agreed to "surrender

00:17:48.752 --> 00:17:51.412
and regrant," he was unknowingly

00:17:51.712 --> 00:17:55.142
converting his clan's shared land into

00:17:55.142 --> 00:17:58.422
his own private estate under English law.

00:17:59.182 --> 00:18:01.582
The people who had always farmed that

00:18:01.582 --> 00:18:04.922
land suddenly had no legal claim to it.

00:18:05.772 --> 00:18:08.202
Disputes over who actually owned

00:18:08.302 --> 00:18:11.062
what would simmer for generations.

00:18:12.202 --> 00:18:16.612
Now, Henry VIII died in 1547, leaving

00:18:16.642 --> 00:18:20.312
Ireland unsettled, only partially subdued,

00:18:20.672 --> 00:18:24.212
and deeply unconvinced by Protestantism.

00:18:25.062 --> 00:18:27.802
His son Edward VI and his daughter

00:18:27.802 --> 00:18:31.352
Mary tried different approaches — Mary,

00:18:31.462 --> 00:18:33.652
who was Catholic, actually started

00:18:33.652 --> 00:18:36.072
the first plantations in the Irish

00:18:36.072 --> 00:18:38.382
midlands, confiscating land from Irish

00:18:38.382 --> 00:18:41.042
lords and giving it to English settlers.

00:18:41.682 --> 00:18:44.502
But it was Henry's other daughter

00:18:44.732 --> 00:18:47.092
who would bring the crisis to a head.

00:18:48.472 --> 00:18:51.012
Elizabeth I decided that the

00:18:51.072 --> 00:18:53.902
time for half-measures was over.

00:18:55.162 --> 00:18:58.432
Under her reign, England committed large

00:18:58.472 --> 00:19:02.602
armies to Ireland with a single goal: full

00:19:02.902 --> 00:19:05.742
military conquest of the entire island,

00:19:06.052 --> 00:19:08.292
not just the management of the Pale.

00:19:09.482 --> 00:19:11.742
This transformed the nature

00:19:11.762 --> 00:19:13.462
of the conflict entirely.

00:19:13.822 --> 00:19:15.122
We are no longer talking about

00:19:15.306 --> 00:19:17.726
diplomacy and land grants.

00:19:17.956 --> 00:19:19.766
We are talking about English

00:19:19.766 --> 00:19:21.776
soldiers stationed all over the

00:19:21.776 --> 00:19:24.676
country, and ultimately, war.

00:19:26.286 --> 00:19:28.846
The most serious of these conflicts — and

00:19:29.056 --> 00:19:31.506
the one that came closest to undoing

00:19:31.526 --> 00:19:33.656
English rule in Ireland entirely

00:19:33.966 --> 00:19:36.026
— was called the Nine Years' War.

00:19:37.386 --> 00:19:41.966
This began in 1593, and was led by a

00:19:41.966 --> 00:19:44.316
man named Hugh O'Neill, who had been

00:19:44.616 --> 00:19:47.166
educated in England, who understood

00:19:47.356 --> 00:19:50.396
English politics from the inside, and who

00:19:50.396 --> 00:19:52.906
held his title from the English Crown.

00:19:53.496 --> 00:19:55.896
An unlikely profile for someone who

00:19:55.896 --> 00:19:57.966
would become the leader of the last

00:19:58.026 --> 00:20:00.536
great Gaelic resistance to English rule.

00:20:01.406 --> 00:20:04.016
Now, O'Neill was patient,

00:20:04.546 --> 00:20:06.786
organised, and extremely effective.

00:20:07.526 --> 00:20:09.606
He trained his men in modern

00:20:09.646 --> 00:20:11.816
European military tactics rather

00:20:11.816 --> 00:20:14.026
than traditional Irish methods.

00:20:14.916 --> 00:20:18.206
And at the Battle of Yellow Ford in

00:20:18.216 --> 00:20:21.656
1598, in the north of Ireland, his

00:20:21.656 --> 00:20:24.736
forces surrounded and destroyed an

00:20:24.736 --> 00:20:27.596
entire English army column, killing

00:20:27.816 --> 00:20:29.536
around two thousand soldiers.

00:20:30.676 --> 00:20:33.646
For a moment, it seemed Ireland

00:20:33.956 --> 00:20:35.706
could actually be lost, or

00:20:35.706 --> 00:20:38.116
rather, won back, for the Irish.

00:20:39.116 --> 00:20:41.596
O’Neill then sought help from

00:20:41.626 --> 00:20:44.156
Elizabeth’s great enemy, Spain,

00:20:44.356 --> 00:20:46.416
which was more than happy to assist.

00:20:47.416 --> 00:20:51.426
In 1601, a Spanish force landed at

00:20:51.426 --> 00:20:53.886
Kinsale, on the south coast of Ireland.

00:20:54.856 --> 00:20:56.886
O’Neill marched his army all the

00:20:56.886 --> 00:20:59.476
way south from Ulster to join them.

00:21:00.296 --> 00:21:03.516
But this was the problem: the Spanish

00:21:03.796 --> 00:21:06.566
had landed far from O’Neill’s power base,

00:21:06.846 --> 00:21:09.906
and before the two forces could properly

00:21:09.906 --> 00:21:12.506
unite, the English moved against them.

00:21:13.346 --> 00:21:15.456
At the Battle of Kinsale, the

00:21:15.456 --> 00:21:17.966
Irish and Spanish were defeated.

00:21:18.746 --> 00:21:20.276
O’Neill submitted to the

00:21:20.286 --> 00:21:22.361
English Crown in 1603.

00:21:23.586 --> 00:21:26.846
And then, in 1607, something

00:21:26.856 --> 00:21:29.166
happened that closed a chapter

00:21:29.356 --> 00:21:31.286
of Irish history for good.

00:21:32.286 --> 00:21:35.186
One autumn morning, Hugh O’Neill and

00:21:35.226 --> 00:21:37.806
other leading Gaelic lords from Ulster,

00:21:38.106 --> 00:21:39.976
along with their families and followers,

00:21:40.566 --> 00:21:43.486
boarded ships and sailed away into

00:21:43.486 --> 00:21:46.796
self-imposed exile, to the more hospitable

00:21:46.846 --> 00:21:49.726
Catholic courts of Spain and Rome.

00:21:51.066 --> 00:21:53.426
Whether they feared arrest or

00:21:53.686 --> 00:21:55.266
were seeking military support from

00:21:55.266 --> 00:21:57.826
abroad, historians still debate.

00:21:58.436 --> 00:22:00.466
But they never came back.

00:22:01.656 --> 00:22:03.366
This event is called

00:22:03.606 --> 00:22:05.676
the Flight of the Earls.

00:22:06.206 --> 00:22:08.196
It marked the end of Gaelic

00:22:08.236 --> 00:22:10.236
Ireland as a political force.

00:22:10.746 --> 00:22:14.056
The old aristocracy — the class that had

00:22:14.066 --> 00:22:16.876
maintained Gaelic culture and resisted

00:22:16.916 --> 00:22:20.316
English rule for centuries — it was gone.

00:22:21.576 --> 00:22:23.906
And the land they left behind

00:22:24.066 --> 00:22:26.016
would not remain empty for long.

00:22:26.976 --> 00:22:28.856
The territories of the fled

00:22:28.856 --> 00:22:31.286
earls — six counties across the

00:22:31.296 --> 00:22:33.276
north of Ireland — were declared

00:22:33.516 --> 00:22:35.506
forfeit to the English Crown.

00:22:36.546 --> 00:22:39.586
And in 1610, the so-called

00:22:39.726 --> 00:22:42.376
Plantation of Ulster began.

00:22:43.396 --> 00:22:46.046
Now, Ulster is the northernmost

00:22:46.196 --> 00:22:48.016
of Ireland's four provinces.

00:22:48.406 --> 00:22:50.356
It had been the most Gaelic, the

00:22:50.356 --> 00:22:52.626
most resistant to English rule.

00:22:53.516 --> 00:22:56.136
Now it was to be transformed.

00:22:57.586 --> 00:22:59.916
The plan was for the land to be

00:22:59.916 --> 00:23:03.096
divided into parcels and distributed

00:23:03.316 --> 00:23:06.186
to settlers from England and Scotland.

00:23:07.286 --> 00:23:09.166
The Gaelic Catholic population

00:23:09.306 --> 00:23:10.546
who had lived on that land for

00:23:10.546 --> 00:23:12.956
centuries was pushed to the

00:23:12.956 --> 00:23:15.326
margins, where farming was harder.

00:23:16.676 --> 00:23:20.696
And by law, settlers were forbidden from

00:23:20.706 --> 00:23:23.216
renting land to native Irish tenants.

00:23:23.996 --> 00:23:26.326
Tens of thousands of settlers

00:23:26.326 --> 00:23:28.316
came, particularly from

00:23:28.316 --> 00:23:29.826
the Lowlands of Scotland.

00:23:30.756 --> 00:23:33.526
Now, Scotland and Ireland

00:23:33.896 --> 00:23:35.236
have a lot in common.

00:23:35.636 --> 00:23:38.116
They are geographically close — you

00:23:38.116 --> 00:23:39.926
can almost see Scotland from the

00:23:39.926 --> 00:23:41.386
northern tip of Ireland on a clear

00:23:41.386 --> 00:23:44.506
day — but in the 17th century,

00:23:44.826 --> 00:23:47.436
Scotland and Ireland, culturally

00:23:47.436 --> 00:23:49.776
speaking, were very different places.

00:23:50.366 --> 00:23:52.411
The Scots were Protestant.

00:23:52.596 --> 00:23:53.616
They spoke English.

00:23:53.806 --> 00:23:55.676
They had no connection to

00:23:55.676 --> 00:23:57.696
Ireland or its traditions.

00:23:58.486 --> 00:24:00.156
The two communities that

00:24:00.156 --> 00:24:01.796
emerged did not mix.

00:24:02.416 --> 00:24:03.526
And of course, they were

00:24:03.526 --> 00:24:04.776
not supposed to mix.

00:24:05.476 --> 00:24:08.936
New towns were built on defensive plans.

00:24:09.336 --> 00:24:11.846
The settlers and the displaced native

00:24:11.846 --> 00:24:14.946
Irish lived side by side, but they

00:24:14.946 --> 00:24:17.386
worshipped in different churches, spoke

00:24:17.396 --> 00:24:20.426
different languages, and saw both the land

00:24:20.466 --> 00:24:22.976
and its future in completely different

00:24:22.976 --> 00:24:26.596
ways: for the settlers, it had been

00:24:26.656 --> 00:24:29.596
lawfully granted by the Crown; for the

00:24:29.606 --> 00:24:32.176
native Irish, it had been taken from them.

00:24:32.996 --> 00:24:36.016
There is a direct, traceable line from

00:24:36.016 --> 00:24:39.411
the decision to colonise Ulster in 1610

00:24:39.816 --> 00:24:42.546
to the partition of Ireland three hundred

00:24:42.546 --> 00:24:45.166
years later — the division of the island

00:24:45.346 --> 00:24:47.556
that you can still see on the map today.

00:24:48.756 --> 00:24:51.846
Now, we've already had a few "hate

00:24:51.856 --> 00:24:54.616
figures", in the form of Henry II and

00:24:54.816 --> 00:24:57.796
Henry VIII, people whose portraits you

00:24:57.796 --> 00:25:00.396
won't find on the wall in many Irish pubs.

00:25:01.056 --> 00:25:04.276
But they pale in comparison to who

00:25:04.276 --> 00:25:06.716
comes next, a man whose name is

00:25:06.726 --> 00:25:08.996
still spoken with bitterness in

00:25:08.996 --> 00:25:11.416
Ireland today: Oliver Cromwell.

00:25:12.766 --> 00:25:15.336
Now, before we get to what he did

00:25:15.556 --> 00:25:17.536
in Ireland, I need to give you

00:25:17.576 --> 00:25:19.426
some context on Oliver Cromwell.

00:25:20.666 --> 00:25:24.326
In England in the 1640s, there had

00:25:24.326 --> 00:25:26.966
been a civil war between King Charles

00:25:26.976 --> 00:25:29.756
I and the Parliament — the body

00:25:29.756 --> 00:25:32.066
that governed alongside the king.

00:25:33.146 --> 00:25:35.546
Cromwell was the military leader

00:25:35.766 --> 00:25:38.166
of the winning Parliamentary side.

00:25:38.906 --> 00:25:43.776
And in 1649, after his victory, he did

00:25:43.776 --> 00:25:46.736
something that shocked Europe: he put

00:25:46.736 --> 00:25:50.246
the king on trial and had him executed.

00:25:51.066 --> 00:25:52.706
By the way, if you would like to hear

00:25:52.706 --> 00:25:56.666
more about that, episode 232 tells that

00:25:56.666 --> 00:25:58.996
story, of when Britain killed its king.

00:26:00.126 --> 00:26:02.856
Anyway, after the execution of

00:26:02.896 --> 00:26:06.076
King Charles I, England became a

00:26:06.076 --> 00:26:09.036
republic, with Cromwell at its head.

00:26:09.856 --> 00:26:12.386
He was a deeply religious Protestant,

00:26:12.496 --> 00:26:14.236
with an absolute conviction

00:26:14.506 --> 00:26:16.346
that he was doing God's work.

00:26:17.496 --> 00:26:19.936
And he did not conceal his

00:26:19.936 --> 00:26:22.366
contempt for Irish Catholicism.

00:26:23.432 --> 00:26:25.602
He believed the Catholic population

00:26:25.602 --> 00:26:27.772
of Ireland was a direct threat

00:26:28.052 --> 00:26:29.682
to England's new republic.

00:26:30.592 --> 00:26:33.712
And so in August 1649, he

00:26:33.772 --> 00:26:36.032
arrived in Ireland with an army

00:26:36.382 --> 00:26:38.132
of around twelve thousand men.

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:41.880
He came to put down a Catholic alliance

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:44.030
and to punish those responsible

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:45.360
for violence against Protestant

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:47.100
settlers eight years earlier.

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:49.850
He was brutally efficient

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:52.860
and utterly without mercy.

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:56.690
At Drogheda — a town north of

00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:59.010
Dublin — the Irish soldiers defending

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:01.230
the town refused to surrender.

00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:04.270
What followed was a massacre.

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:07.280
Several thousand people were killed:

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:10.480
soldiers, civilians, and Catholic

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:12.730
clergy sheltering inside the town.

00:27:13.653 --> 00:27:15.863
Cromwell described it in a letter

00:27:15.863 --> 00:27:18.833
home as God's righteous judgement.

00:27:19.593 --> 00:27:21.233
The following month, a similar

00:27:21.233 --> 00:27:22.893
thing happened at Wexford.

00:27:23.393 --> 00:27:24.733
Thousands more died.

00:27:26.103 --> 00:27:29.453
Cromwell left Ireland in 1650, but

00:27:29.673 --> 00:27:31.673
the campaign continued for another

00:27:31.683 --> 00:27:33.603
three years under his officers.

00:27:34.203 --> 00:27:36.213
The human cost was devastating.

00:27:36.863 --> 00:27:38.323
Hundreds of thousands of people

00:27:38.323 --> 00:27:41.753
died — from battle, from the famines

00:27:41.873 --> 00:27:43.613
that followed the destruction of

00:27:43.613 --> 00:27:45.933
the countryside, and from disease.

00:27:46.603 --> 00:27:48.793
A population of perhaps one and a

00:27:48.793 --> 00:27:52.503
half million in 1641 may have fallen

00:27:52.663 --> 00:27:55.769
to fewer than a million by 1652.

00:27:56.988 --> 00:27:59.438
The land settlement that followed was

00:27:59.588 --> 00:28:02.038
as brutal as the military campaign.

00:28:02.848 --> 00:28:05.588
Catholic landowners lost their estates.

00:28:06.308 --> 00:28:08.778
Those who survived were ordered west

00:28:08.818 --> 00:28:11.668
of the River Shannon — to Connacht, the

00:28:11.798 --> 00:28:14.098
western province of Ireland, the most

00:28:14.108 --> 00:28:16.458
remote and poorest part of the island.

00:28:17.528 --> 00:28:19.588
And there's a famous phrase that

00:28:19.608 --> 00:28:21.968
entered Irish historical memory from

00:28:21.968 --> 00:28:25.038
this period, "To Hell or to Connacht".

00:28:26.304 --> 00:28:30.614
Before 1641, Catholics had owned roughly

00:28:30.864 --> 00:28:32.934
sixty percent of the land in Ireland.

00:28:33.434 --> 00:28:35.954
By 1660, they owned an

00:28:35.964 --> 00:28:37.664
estimated twenty percent.

00:28:38.534 --> 00:28:41.714
And the Cromwellian settlement created

00:28:41.724 --> 00:28:43.994
the social structure that would define

00:28:43.994 --> 00:28:46.434
Ireland for the next two centuries.

00:28:47.234 --> 00:28:48.904
A Protestant landowning

00:28:48.904 --> 00:28:50.764
class sat at the top.

00:28:51.454 --> 00:28:54.184
A Catholic majority of tenants and

00:28:54.184 --> 00:28:57.304
workers lived beneath them, on land

00:28:57.514 --> 00:28:59.804
that had once been their own, or their

00:28:59.814 --> 00:29:01.894
parents', or their grandparents'.

00:29:02.374 --> 00:29:03.538
Everyone knew it.

00:29:03.884 --> 00:29:06.264
The awareness of that dispossession

00:29:06.544 --> 00:29:08.664
ran like a fault line through

00:29:08.714 --> 00:29:09.804
everything that followed.

00:29:11.154 --> 00:29:13.654
Almost five centuries earlier, the

00:29:13.654 --> 00:29:16.124
exiled King Diarmait had invited

00:29:16.124 --> 00:29:17.954
the English over to Ireland.

00:29:18.504 --> 00:29:20.814
It would prove to be a decision that

00:29:20.814 --> 00:29:23.514
would forever change the country, and

00:29:23.514 --> 00:29:25.714
one that the people of Ireland would

00:29:25.714 --> 00:29:28.894
spend the next 800 years trying to undo.

00:29:29.824 --> 00:29:32.334
The story of what comes next — the

00:29:32.364 --> 00:29:34.354
Act of Union, the Great Famine,

00:29:34.594 --> 00:29:36.634
the Civil War, and the road to the

00:29:36.634 --> 00:29:38.954
Republic — that is what we have

